"You'll own nothing and be happy" - [World Economic Forum](https://www.weforum.org)
You'll own nothing and be happy" is a phrase published by the World Economic Forum (WEF) and based on a 2016 essay by the Danish politician Ida Auken
about a future in which a hypothetical person relies on the sharing economy for many of their needs. (Wikipedia)

This phrase gets more accurate, day by day.

# The End Of Ownership

Imagine this: It's 2002 and your favorite singer has just released their new song. You pop into a store and get their new CD, with their whole album on it.
**You own this CD.**
You can always play this CD, as long as you have a CD player. It doesn't matter if you're in Tokyo, or in the Antarctic, you can play your song.
These days are over. Today, we are mostly buying licenses.

## You don't own the game, you own the temporary permission to play.

Have you ever bought a game digitally? If yes, then congratulations, you fell victim to the new age of "renting".
Every time, you buy a game digitally, no matter if you do it on the PlayStation Store, Steam or even the PlayStore, you're not buying the game, but a license instead.
Most famous example of this is the first itteration of "The Crew" series. In March of 2024, Ubisoft made the decision to permanently shut down servers,
as well as taking away the game from peoples library, technically killing their license.

